This is a fundamental shift in perspective. Pausing to re-examine the core physical formulation and data assimilation architecture is the right move. The verification metrics clearly show why this step back is necessary:

1. **Mass Loss/Pressure Collapse:** $\text{BIAS}_p = -567.36\text{ hPa/Pa}$, with ACC for pressure stuck at $\approx 0.17–0.18$ across all vertical levels.
2. **Geostrophic Disconnect:** Wind components $u$ and $v$ have extreme relative errors ($156\%$ and $193\%$) and poor ACC ($\sim 0.1–0.3$), indicating that the GNN predictions lack large-scale geostrophic coupling with the mass field ($T$ and $P/\rho$).
3. **Density Scaling Artifacts:** Variable $q$ shows a massive relative difference ($461\%$) because absolute perturbations across vertical levels were not log-scaled or normalized to background density profiles.

By shifting from standard pressure-coordinate hydrostatic assumptions to a **height-based ($z$-coordinate) non-hydrostatic formulation using logarithmic density ($\ln\rho$) and log-temperature ($\ln T$)**, we solve the scale-disparity problem in the vertical.

